From 8b5c0129327dbda02c5af3d34f6694b6e786c904 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 16:12:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] GSXib5KeyedUnarchiver: give a xib's formatters their settings

A numberFormatter or dateFormatter element carries its settings as
attributes (formatterBehavior="custom10_4" numberStyle="decimal"
generatesDecimalNumbers="YES" maximumFractionDigits="3", or
dateStyle="short" timeStyle="short") and child elements
(<real key="minimum" value="0.0"/>).  NSNumberFormatter and
NSDateFormatter take those settings from the NS.attributes dictionary of
a keyed archive, as a compiled nib carries it, and nothing presented a
xib's attributes under that key, so every formatter in a xib came out
with the defaults.

NS.attributes is now decoded from the element: each attribute under its
own name, but for id, key, class, customClass and userLabel, with
Interface Builder's names for the behaviors, number styles, padding
positions, rounding modes and date and time styles turned into their
values, digit counts and widths into numbers and YES and NO into
booleans; and each child element with a key, decoded as usual.  The
names are the ones ibtool accepts; "10_0" is the 10.0 behavior.  A date
formatter gets the 10.4 behavior whatever the xib says, as macOS gives
it (its format is then an ICU pattern).

The formatters take the settings with the libs-base change that makes
them read NS.attributes.  Tests/gui/GSXib5KeyedUnarchiver/formatters.m
loads Formatters.xib and checks the values macOS gives for it, compiled
by ibtool; with that libs-base change, 19 of its 28 checks fail without
this one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 Source/GSXib5KeyedUnarchiver.m                | 119 +++++++++++++++++
 .../gui/GSXib5KeyedUnarchiver/Formatters.xib  |  64 +++++++++
